---
import InsetText from "@components/inset-text/inset-text.astro";
import Tag from "@components/tag/tag.astro";
import Layout from "@layouts/DecisionsRecordLayout.astro";
---

<Layout>
    <h1 class="govuk-heading-xl">002 - Buttons</h1>
    <p class="govuk-body">Date updated: <strong>19 August 2026</strong></p>
    <Tag color="green">Accepted</Tag>
    <br />
    <br />
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Context</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        As mentioned in the <a
            href="/decisions-record/adapt-govuk-design-system"
            class="govuk-link">001 - Adapt the GOV.UK Design System</a
        > decision record, the GOV.UK Design System was not designed for web apps
        and as a result inputs, including buttons, have been designed to draw the
        attention of the reader and not be msised when scanning a form to be filled
        in.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        The advice on buttons from GOV.UK Design System is to:
    </p>

    <InsetText>
        <i
            >Before adding lots of secondary buttons, try to simplify the page
            or break the content down across multiple pages.
        </i>
    </InsetText>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        However, in the context of web apps which are designed to be used
        multiple times, splitting content to different pages often leads to a
        less user-friendly experience.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        There are two primary scenarios in which the GOV.UK buttons are not
        suitable:
    </p>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--number govuk-list--spaced">
        <li>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                <strong>Scenario 1:</strong>
                When buttons are not the primary action of the page, the attention
                of the user is drawn to the buttons and they are more likely to be
                clicked or confused at what they are meant to do.
            </p>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                In the example below, you can see the <strong>Rename</strong> and
                <strong>Delete</strong> strongly draw the eye whereas the primary
                action is actually to click on the title and view the item in full.
            </p>
            <img
                src="/images/non-primary-action-buttons.png"
                alt="Screenshot an item with link and a rename and delete button."
                class="govuk-image"
            />
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                <strong>Scenario 2:</strong>
                When there are multiple buttons on the page, the buttons are difficult
                to distinguish from each other and quickly navigate between actions.
            </p>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                Again the primary action of this page is to read the text
                content below, your attention to the buttons should only be
                drawn when needed. E.g. User first decides they want to download
                the summary, they then look for the download button. As opposed
                to reading all options first and remembering which one they
                wanted to click.
            </p>
            <img
                src="/images/multiple-secondary-buttons.webp"
                alt="Screenshot a page with multiple buttons."
                class="govuk-image"
            />
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Accessibility concerns</h3>
    <p class="govuk-body"></p>
</Layout>
